\maketitle

% ==========================================
% ABSTRACT
% ==========================================
\begin{abstract}
Low-cost flash storage serves as the default persistence layer for many embedded hardware platforms. When subjected to continuous write-intensive workloads, their limited program--erase endurance often functions as the dominant reliability constraint. Default Linux configurations emit fragmented, high-frequency streams of small logical writes that interact poorly with the consumer-grade Flash Translation Layers (FTLs) embedded in these devices. Because consumer microcontrollers generally provide minimal over-provisioning space, this structural mismatch severely accelerates logical write amplification, ultimately forcing premature storage media failure.

To rigorously address this operational limitation, this study structurally analyzes the storage degradation incurred within 32GB SD cards operating under continuous, high-volume write conditions. We execute a coordinated set of kernel- and filesystem-level configurations analytically designed to reduce destructive write amplification via forced serial batching. By logically combining ZRAM swap compression, extreme page-cache aggregation ceilings, deterministic multiqueue NOOP block scheduling, and migrating storage architecture from legacy Ext4 to the log-structured F2FS filesystem, the total system Write Amplification Factor (WAF) successfully decreased from a destructive 12.43 down to a stable 2.10. Consequently, absolute empirical physical writes declined from 4.2GB down to approximately 0.8GB per daily cycle.

Injecting these reduced operational parameters into a verified JEDEC-aligned endurance projection model extends projected hardware lifespan by a factor of nearly thirty. Furthermore, this optimized host-side topography significantly suppresses extreme hardware tail-latency spikes associated with failing garbage collection (GC) sequences in aging filesystem spaces. Our empirical evaluations establish that correctly architected host-level I/O manipulation reliably addresses storage endurance constraints across unmonitored write-intensive embedded environments.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Flash Storage, Write Amplification, Embedded Systems, Kernel Optimization, F2FS, NAND Flash, Wear Leveling, Garbage Collection.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

% ==========================================
% I. INTRODUCTION
% ==========================================
\section{Introduction}

Modern embedded deployments extensively integrate inexpensive flash media devices to maintain scalable deployment parameters and ensure simplified field replacement protocols. Among these commercial options, Secure Digital (SD) cards persist natively because they guarantee unparalleled cost density and absolute structural hardware compatibility footprint metrics.

Despite overwhelming ease of use, standard SD cards structurally lack the fundamental hardware engineering required to reliably sustain ultra-high-frequency write permutations commonly emitted by continuous automated workloads. The confluence of heavy daemon telemetry generation, containerized microservice swapping, and aggressive cache write-backs establishes a severe I/O topology that aggressively damages low-tier flash controllers restricted by constrained over-provisioning buffers \cite{b26}. Executed persistently over trailing continuous timelines, this specific write dynamic systematically fractures available generic erase block thresholds, inevitably truncating media lifespan and invoking systemic hardware faults \cite{b10}.

Operating within rigid boundary limits characterizing unattended embedded units—components lacking RAM redundancies or uninterruptible power constraints—requires isolated optimization architectures expressly manipulating storage reliability divorced entirely from sweeping scalable cloud orchestration assumptions \cite{b30}. 

This study distinctly addresses storage endurance constraints via operating system configuration boundaries rather than invasive proprietary FTL firmware hacking. Utilizing robust systemic empirical measurement, the operational optimizations follow:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Mathematical Workload Isolation:} We actively define absolute Write Amplification Factor (WAF) models framing the destructive constraints incurred processing write-intensive embedded metrics on generic hardware limits.
    \item \textbf{Kernel-Level Configuration Models:} We rigidly manipulate Linux VFS page-cache state logic alongside integrated ZRAM and defined NOOP sorting queues, expressly converting continuous random trickle-writes into batched continuous sequential I/O arrays.
    \item \textbf{Filesystem Geometric Analysis:} We demonstrate definitively how abandoning the legacy Ext4 JBD2 journal penalty safely eliminates structural physical storage amplification cycles natively.
    \item \textbf{Lifespan Equation Grounding:} Incorporating empirically measured kernel tracing parameters strictly into JEDEC-aligned formulas verifies realistic hardware extensions matching targeted continuous operational life cycles.
\end{enumerate}

