\section{NAND Flash Mechanics and FTL Modeling}

Formulating an operational solution directly addressing memory cell expiration structurally requires deconstructing foundational physical boundaries limiting NAND geometric media logic.

\subsection{Wear Dynamics and Page/Block Asymmetry}

Flash structures are inherently arranged across a rigid fixed-size hierarchical grid topology spanning geometric storage matrices. Standard structural mapping establishes granular \textit{pages} (typically bound between 4KB generating 16KB limits) mathematically packed into aggregate \textit{blocks} (scaling between 128 through 256 physical pages). Foundational constraints limiting continuous memory usage generate explicitly via core physical asymmetries: Data operates read and program protocols highly efficiently upon isolated page-level footprints, yet the underlying geometric strata unconditionally requires absolute erasure execution scaled entirely across the vastly larger macro block footprint.

Under operational laws preventing isolated page overwrites, modifying embedded parameter bytes structurally demands shifting incoming payloads toward separate, erased target nodes whilst formally flagging legacy source boundaries strictly invalid.

Wear limits trace explicitly back tracking Fowler-Nordheim active tunneling forces tearing deeply across floating gate isolation oxides throughout cyclic program-erase loops. Trapping anomalous electrical charges incrementally shifts absolute structural voltage parameter thresholds, generating narrowed read capacity dependencies pushing heavy mathematical loads demanding persistent embedded Error Correction Code (ECC) recovery thresholds \cite{b6}. Reaching catastrophic uncorrectable shifts mandates formal block retirement.

\subsection{FTL Translation Limits and FTL Isolation}

As host environments interact transparently communicating exclusively addressing Logical Block Addresses (LBA), the internal embedded Flash Translation Layer (FTL) retains exclusive dictation routing geometric mapping logic defining true wear outcomes. 

Massive disparities isolating logical OS requests dividing physical array movements functionally define severe Write Amplification logic. Injecting thousands of highly fractured generic kilobyte transactions aggressively triggers massive Read-Modify-Write (RMW) cyclic loop sequences directly taxing FTL microprocessors forcing invalid block harvesting sweeps physically executing megabyte erasure arrays \cite{b7}. 

Enterprise NVMe standards actively mask fragmentation utilizing gigabyte DRAM tracking banks combined effectively with heavy hardware structural reserves; inversely, consumer SD boundaries operate constrained cost-optimized firmware leaving internal flash geometries unconditionally unprotected against volatile trickle writes.

\subsection{Mathematical FTL GC Isolation Dynamics}

Masking synchronous RMW overhead execution logic effectively tasks FTL programming utilizing pre-erased sector allocations passively masking write commands until spare segments eventually saturate forcing aggressive deterministic background Garbage Collection (GC) logic sweeping victim blocks.

Efficiency margins resolving isolated GC passes scale directly alongside explicitly designated spare capacity \cite{b27}. Desnoyers' mathematical framework evaluates Greedy GC algorithms determining theoretical random write scaling mapping Write Amplification metrics scaling functionally relying tightly upon defined analytical Over-Provisioning geometric thresholds designated $\alpha$ formally expressing spare operational capacity \cite{b8}.

Theoretical physical degradation scales exponentially establishing parameters:
\begin{equation}
WAF_{FTL} \approx \frac{1 + \alpha}{\alpha}
\end{equation}

Empirical consumer class constraints measure aggressive physical under-provisioning marking parameters strictly $\alpha \approx 0.05$ marking minimal thresholds natively exposing operations directly forcing accelerated wear profiles barring explicit protective host-side intervention geometries successfully clustering inputs natively \cite{b25}.

